  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1229 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nHigh pressure/mid-level ridging will remain in place through the \nend of the week with above average temperatures and dry weather \nensuing. Moisture and precipitation associated with tropical \nsystem Isaias is likely to remain east of our area as it moves \ninland over the weekend. In its wake, a ridge of high pressure \nwill strengthen over the central and southern plains into early \nnext week. With this in mind, temperatures will remain nearly \nsteady over the next several days (mid-upper 80s, lower 90s). \nAfternoon winds will become increasingly breezy by this weekend \ninto early next week, which may tend to increase the fire weather \nthreat again. Portions of NE OK which missed out on the heaviest \nrains last week will be most susceptible (W of Hwy 75).\n\nThe synoptic pattern changes by mid next week as troughing over \nthe western US moves eastward. A series of cold fronts are \nprojected to impact the area Tuesday through late week with \nincreasing rain/thunderstorm potential as well as falling \ntemperatures. By this time, average temps run in the mid 70s, so \neven just cooling back down to near average would be welcome. \nThe Climate Prediction Center continues to indicate above average\ntemperatures are favored through mid-late month overall.",
